1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,872
The brush uncovers a standing slab, carved with a figure and a few lines of writing.

2
00:00:05,872 --> 00:00:12,372
It is a hero stone, set up for someone who died fighting — often, the inscriptions say, defending cattle.

3
00:00:19,867 --> 00:00:26,523
Hundreds of these survive across the south, and they are among the earliest inscribed objects in the Tamil country.

4
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:30,190
Four sources, unusually.

5
00:00:30,190 --> 00:00:32,098
A large body of Tamil poetry.

6
00:00:32,098 --> 00:00:35,724
Early Tamil-Brahmi inscriptions in caves and on pottery.

7
00:00:35,724 --> 00:00:38,777
Excavation at sites like Arikamedu and Keeladi.

8
00:00:38,777 --> 00:00:46,519
And classical accounts from the Mediterranean, which describe the ports in detail because their merchants used them.

9
00:00:49,933 --> 00:00:53,162
Three dynasties, the muvendar, the three crowned kings.

10
00:00:53,162 --> 00:00:59,446
The Cheras in the west, in what is now Kerala, with their emblem the bow and their ports on the Arabian Sea.

11
00:00:59,446 --> 00:01:07,586
The Cholas in the central east, emblem the tiger, with Uraiyur as a capital and Puhar, also called Kaveripattinam, as their great port.

12
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:16,732
The Pandyas in the far south, emblem the fish, with Madurai as capital and Korkai their pearl port.

13
00:01:16,732 --> 00:01:20,982
Learn the three with their emblems and you have the backbone of this unit.

14
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:29,237
These were trading states, and the trade was long-distance.

15
00:01:29,237 --> 00:01:33,677
Pepper, pearls, ivory, fine cotton and precious stones went west.

16
00:01:33,677 --> 00:01:36,063
Roman gold and wine came east.

17
00:01:40,333 --> 00:01:44,506
Puhar and Muziris appear in both Tamil poems and Mediterranean sources,

18
00:01:44,506 --> 00:01:49,721
and at Arikamedu near Puducherry the excavation turns up Mediterranean pottery and glass.

19
00:01:49,721 --> 00:01:54,020
This is one of the places where a text and the ground agree exactly.

20
00:01:58,533 --> 00:02:01,261
And the poetry organises the land itself.

21
00:02:01,261 --> 00:02:09,770
Tamil poetic convention divides the country into five tinais, landscapes, each with its own terrain, people, occupations and mood:

22
00:02:09,770 --> 00:02:17,799
kurinji the hills, mullai the forest and pasture, marudam the farmland, neidal the coast, and palai the dry wasteland.

23
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:28,050
It is a classification of ecology and of feeling at once, and nothing quite like it exists elsewhere in early Indian literature.

24
00:02:31,367 --> 00:02:35,078
The poems describe a society of kings and smaller chieftains,

25
00:02:35,078 --> 00:02:40,287
of bards and poets who travelled between patrons, of cattle raids and of markets.

26
00:02:45,633 --> 00:02:49,477
Patronage is a constant theme: poets praise generous patrons by name,

27
00:02:49,477 --> 00:02:54,253
and complain about mean ones, which is why we know as many chieftains' names as we do.

28
00:02:54,253 --> 00:03:01,197
The hero stones belong to this world — a society where fighting over cattle was common enough to need its own monuments.

29
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:12,775
Muziris, the Chera port the Roman sources name, is identified with a site near Kodungallur in Kerala.

30
00:03:15,500 --> 00:03:18,022
Puhar is also called Kaveripattinam.

31
00:03:18,022 --> 00:03:19,795
Madurai is still Madurai.

32
00:03:19,795 --> 00:03:24,049
Questions like to give you one name and expect the other.

33
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:29,904
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

34
00:03:29,904 --> 00:03:33,709
Chera, Chola, Pandya — bow, tiger, fish.

35
00:03:33,709 --> 00:03:35,772
West, east, south.

36
00:03:40,467 --> 00:03:41,246
Dig Quiz.

37
00:03:41,246 --> 00:03:42,726
Pause and name it.

38
00:03:42,726 --> 00:03:47,634
First: the dynasty of the west coast whose emblem was the bow.

39
00:03:47,634 --> 00:03:48,869
The Cheras.

40
00:03:57,833 --> 00:04:03,459
Second: the dynasty of the far south, emblem the fish, whose pearl port was Korkai.

41
00:04:03,459 --> 00:04:04,330
The Pandyas.

42
00:04:04,330 --> 00:04:10,523
Third: the site near Puducherry where excavation found Mediterranean pottery and glass.

43
00:04:12,567 --> 00:04:13,302
Arikamedu.

44
00:04:13,302 --> 00:04:20,354
In Mains, a standard question asks what Sangam literature tells us about early south Indian society.

45
00:04:23,433 --> 00:04:32,419
Use the five landscapes, the patronage system, the trade, and the hero stones — and note that poetry idealises, so read it carefully.

46
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:42,050
A hero stone takes its place in the gallery, set up for somebody whose name we would not otherwise have.

47
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,339
Next gallery: the poetry itself — who wrote it, how it survived, and what it is actually about.

48
00:04:52,339 --> 00:04:56,403
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

